MINIMAL A Step-by-Step Speech Practice Guide For people who experience occasional or relatively light stammering. How to use this guide Practice calmly and consistently. The aim is not to force words out or speak unnaturally slowly. Use the exercises to develop relaxed breathing, controlled speech, comfortable vowel sounds, and confidence in everyday conversations. TIMING & COMMITMENT: Best time to practice: Early morning (ideally 5:00–7:00 AM) • Your mind is fresh and calm • Sets a positive tone for your entire day • Builds confidence before daily interactions • No interruptions or distractions Duration: 6 months of consistent practice • Weeks 1–2: Exercises feel new, progress is fast • Weeks 3–8: Habits form, breathing becomes automatic • Weeks 9–24: Real-world improvements show, confidence builds • Month 6+: Changes become permanent, speech feels natural The 6-month commitment is essential. Stammering patterns took years to form. Rebuilding takes consistent practice. Do not expect overnight results, but expect steady, measurable improvement by month 3. Important: This is an educational self-practice guide, not a medical treatment or guaranteed cure. If stammering is persistent, worsening, causing significant distress, or substantially interfering with school, work, or relationships, consider working with a qualified speech-language pathologist/speech therapist. Daily sequence (Morning routine — 20–25 minutes) BEST TIME: Early morning (5:00–7:00 AM) when your mind is calm 1. Breathing (3 min) 20 slow nose breaths + 11 alternating-nostril repetitions2. Vocal exercises (8–10 min) 15 words for each vowel sound, with 5–7 second vowel stretching 3. Slow reading (7–10 min) Read 4–5 words per comfortable mouth-inhale, using gentle vowel stretching 4. Subconscious rewiring (2–3 min) Focus on relaxation. Say: 'I am building a new pattern. My voice is calm and strong.' 5. Night routine (Before bed) Read your journal + repeat a positive framework before sleep DURATION: Continue this daily sequence for 6 months minimum • Consistency matters more than perfection • Skip a day? No problem. Resume the next day. • Stick with it. Changes are permanent after 6 months.STEP 1 — BREATHING EXERCISES The first goal is to make your breathing slow, steady, and relaxed before you begin speaking. A. Slow nasal breathing — 20 repetitions 1. Sit or stand comfortably and relax your shoulders. 2. Inhale slowly through your nose. 3. Exhale slowly through your nose. 4. Keep the breath comfortable. Do not force a deep breath. 5. Repeat 20 times. Practice cue: slow in → slow out → relaxed body. B. Alternating-nostril breathing — 11 repetitions 1. Close your left nostril gently with your finger. 2. Inhale slowly through your right nostril. 3. Release the left nostril and gently close the right nostril. 4. Exhale slowly through the left nostril. 5. Continue the pattern for 11 repetitions. 6. Keep the breathing comfortable rather than trying to take very large breaths. If you feel dizzy, uncomfortable, or short of breath, stop and return to normal breathing.STEP 2 — VOCAL EXERCISES Take a comfortable breath through your mouth, then gently stretch the target vowel for about 5–7 seconds before completing the word. Example: apple → aaaaaaaapple Do not squeeze your throat or push the voice. The stretch should feel gentle and controlled. Vowel A — 15 words aaaaaaapple, aaaaaaanimal, aaaaaaanswer, aaaaaaartist, aaaaaaafternoon, aaaaaaairport, aaaaaaactor, aaaaaaaddress, aaaaaaadventure, aaaaaaangel, aaaaaaapron, aaaaaaanchor, aaaaaaavocado, aaaaaaarrow, aaaaaaautumn Vowel E — 15 words eeeeeeegg, eeeeeeeleephant, eeeeeeenergy, eeeeeeengine, eeeeeeevening, eeeeeeeagle, eeeeeeearth, eeeeeeecho, eeeeeeelbow, eeeeeeeenvelope, eeeeeeexercise, eeeeeeentry, eeeeeeevent, eeeeeeexample, eeeeeeelevator Vowel I — 15 words iiiiiiice, iiiiiiidea, iiiiiiisland, iiiiiiimage, iiiiiiinside, iiiiiiinsect, iiiiiiinvite, iiiiiiimportant, iiiiiiinterest, iiiiiiinternet, iiiiiiinstant, iiiiiiincome, iiiiiiinjury, iiiiiiitem, iiiiiiimagine Vowel O — 15 words ooooooorange, ooooooocean, ooooooopen, oooooooffice, ooooooorder, ooooooonly, ooooooowner, oooooooasis, ooooooobject, oooooooutside, ooooooovertime, oooooooption, oooooooval, ooooooonion, oooooooriginal Vowel U — 15 words uuuuuuumbrella, uuuuuuuncle, uuuuuuunder, uuuuuuunit, uuuuuuuse, uuuuuuusual, uuuuuuuniverse, uuuuuuunique, uuuuuuuniform, uuuuuuup, uuuuuuupper, uuuuuuurgent, uuuuuuuseful, uuuuuuunderstand, uuuuuuupdateSTEP 3 — SLOW READING PRACTICE Now transfer the controlled breathing and vowel practice into connected speech. How to practice 1. Choose a short paragraph, story, article, or book. 2. Take a comfortable inhale through your mouth. 3. Read approximately 4–5 words on that breath. 4. Use the same gentle vowel-stretching technique while saying the words, aiming for approximately 5–6 seconds on the stretched vowel when practicing. 5. Pause, breathe comfortably, and continue. 6. Focus on smoothness and relaxation rather than speed. Example rhythm Breathe → read 4–5 words slowly → pause → breathe → read the next 4–5 words. Important: The purpose is controlled practice, not permanently speaking every word with a long 5–6 second stretch. As the exercise becomes comfortable, speech should gradually become more natural.STEP 4 — SUBCONSCIOUS REWIRING Speech practice is not only about the mouth and breathing. You can also train your attention by deliberately noticing successful speaking experiences and replacing old negative expectations with new, realistic experiences. Daily process 1. Complete the breathing, vocal, and reading exercises. 2. Speak with a friend or family member in a normal, relaxed conversation. 3. Do not obsessively monitor every word. Focus on communicating your message. 4. If you notice a conversation in which you spoke comfortably or experienced less stammering, write it down in your journal. 5. Record what happened: Who did I speak with? What did I talk about? What felt easier? 6. Read your successful moments every night before sleeping. Subconscious thought framework Use this framework as a reminder: "I am eliminating the hold that my past bad speaking experiences have had on me. I am creating new, positive speaking experiences by learning and practicing a correct, structured path. Every successful conversation gives my mind a new experience to learn from. I do not need to erase my past; I can learn from it and build a new pattern through consistent practice." Journal example "Today I spoke with my brother about my day. I stayed relaxed and was able to say what I wanted to say. I noticed that I did not stammer much during the conversation." The goal of the journal is to collect real experiences—not to pretend that every conversation will be perfectly fluent.STEP 5 — NIGHT-TIME POSITIVE FRAMEWORK Before sleeping, read your journal and repeat a supportive statement. Keep the statement realistic and focused on your actions and progress. Choose one or more: • "I am doing my part to improve my speech, one step at a time." • "I am practicing consistently and becoming more comfortable with speaking." • "I do not need to rush my words. I can communicate calmly." • "I am learning a better path to speaking and I am practicing it every day." • "My past experiences do not have to determine every future conversation. I am creating new experiences through practice." Night routine: Read your journal → breathe comfortably → repeat your chosen framework → sleep.STEP 5.5 — THE 6-MONTH COMMITMENT ROADMAP Month 1: Foundation Building • Breathing becomes your natural reflex • Vocal exercises feel less awkward • You notice small improvements in one-on-one conversations • Goal: Exercises feel routine Month 2: Growth & Awareness • Speech feels smoother when reading aloud • You catch yourself using breathing automatically during the day • Journal entries show more successful speaking moments • Goal: Confidence in familiar situations Month 3: Real Progress • Noticeable reduction in stammering (30–40%) • Comfortable speaking in small group conversations • Less anxiety before speaking • Goal: Believe that change is possible Month 4: Building Momentum • Speak more fluently in work/school situations • Make phone calls without excessive anxiety • Notice others responding positively to your calmer speech • Goal: Use speech in new situations Month 5: Integration • Speaking feels less like 'practice' and more like habit • Stammering occurs less frequently • Confidence extends to new situations (presentations, meetings) • Goal: Speech becomes automatic Month 6: Permanence • Changes feel permanent and natural • Stammering is significantly reduced (60–80%) • Confidence in most speaking situations • Goal: Celebrate 6 months of consistency What to expect: Progress is not linear. Some days will be easier than others. Stress may temporarily increase stammering. Use breathing exercises. After month 3, you will want to continue. The improvement motivates you. After month 6, the changes are yours to keep. Keep going. The 6-month investment pays dividends for life.STEP 6 — RECOMMENDED VIDEOS These resources are included as supplementary learning. Watch them to learn communication, confidence, mindset, and the lived experience of stuttering. They are not substitutes for individualized speech therapy. Speaking & confidence 1. Julian Treasure — "How to Speak So That People Want to Listen" A highly viewed TED Talk covering vocal exercises, powerful speaking, and empathy. 2. Celeste Headlee — "10 Ways to Have a Better Conversation" Practical principles for listening and having better conversations. 3. Amy Cuddy — "Your Body Language May Shape Who You Are" A talk about body language and feelings of confidence. 4. Manish Upadhyay — "A Journey From Stutter to Trainer" A TEDx talk from a speaker describing his own experience with stammering. 5. LeRon Barton — "How I Overcame My Fear of Stuttering" A personal TEDx account focused on fear of stuttering and speaking publicly. Mind training & mental practice 6. Andy Puddicombe — "All It Takes Is 10 Mindful Minutes" A TED talk introducing a short mindfulness practice for attention and present-moment awareness. 7. TED playlist — "Best TED Talks on Mindset and Positive Thinking" A curated collection covering practical topics such as mindfulness, stress, resilience, and mental habits. 8. Dr. Jeetendra Adhia — "Unleash the Power of Subconscious Mind" A TEDx talk specifically focused on subconscious-mind ideas. Suggested viewing order: Julian Treasure → Manish Upadhyay → LeRon Barton → Celeste Headlee → Andy Puddicombe.Final reminder The objective is not to demand perfect fluency from yourself. Build a calmer relationship with speaking, practice consistently, notice real improvements, and continue communicating even when speech is not perfect.